    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 052136\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n236 PM MST Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures with minimal day to day changes will\n  exist through the start of the week as afternoon highs reach 97\n  to 103 degrees across the lower deserts.\n\n- An Extreme Heat Watch is in effect for portions of southeast\n  California and southwest Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday as\n  temperatures warm to near record levels and lead to Major heat\n  risk.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through Saturday\n  with shower and storm chances returning to AZ as early as \n  Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \n\nIt has been another breezy day across southern AZ, but winds have\nnot quite as strong as yesterday due to a weakening pressure \ngradient. Surface pressure to the east is lowering which is \nallowing the east-west gradient to relax. This weakening will \ncontinue through tomorrow morning. So, there will still be some \nbreeziness in the morning tomorrow before the gradient fully \nrelaxes and winds return to a more typical light diurnal pattern.\n\nHigh pressure remains in place over the Southwest, with midday\nobjective analysis showing H5 heights around 591dam, which is\njust above the 90th percentile based on sounding climatology. \nThis high pressure, and mostly clear skies, will continue to \nsupport above normal temperatures across the region. Latest \naircraft soundings out of Phoenix show little change in the low-\nlevel thermal profile over the last 24 hours. So, high \ntemperatures this afternoon will be nearly identical to yesterday,\nwith lower deserts topping out around 97-103 degrees.\n\nHeading through tomorrow and Wednesday a weak shortwave - \ncurrently over northern NV - is expected to turn southeast and \ndrop through northeast AZ on Wednesday while a strong anticyclone \ndevelops near the coast of SoCal. While the shortwave may lead to \nisolated showers and storms up along the Mogollon Rim Wednesday, \nthe lower deserts are expected to remain dry. The shortwave will \nalso help keep south-central AZ from seeing a significant warm-up,\nunlike areas to the west. Temperatures tomorrow will still be \nsimilar to today, if not a degree warmer, and then temperatures \nwarm 2-4 degrees into Wednesday, with nearly all lower desert \nlocations reaching 100 degrees in the afternoon. Temperatures \nwill be hottest across southeast CA and along the Lower Colorado \nRiver Valley, including Yuma, where highs on Wednesday will reach \nthe 104-107 degree range, which is upwards of 11-14 degrees above\nnormal for early October and will be near daily records. An\nExtreme Heat Watch remains in effect starting Wednesday for the\nImperial Valley, CA and Yuma, AZ.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \n\nA strong anticyclone is expected to persist near the SoCal coast\nthrough at least Thursday, which will keep temperatures well above\nnormal and near daily records across SoCal. Temperatures are also\nexpected to warm another 1-2 degrees across south-central AZ on\nThursday, following the passage of a weak shortwave trough. Even\nsouth-central AZ will see high temperatures up to 10 degrees above\nnormal (99-102 degrees) on Thursday. An Extreme Heat Watch \nremains in effect through Thursday for Imperial Valley, CA and \nYuma, AZ, where probabilities of Major HeatRisk are highest \n(50-70%). This watch is likely to be upgraded to a warning tonight\nor tomorrow morning and Friday is being considered for the heat\nalert as well. Latest NBM forecast shows 30-50% Major HeatRisk \nprobs on Friday for the same areas under the current watch. The \ncoastal anticyclone is expected to breakdown heading into Friday \nas the synoptic pattern shifts, with a Pacific low moving toward \nthe CA coast, but low level temperatures are expected to remain \nvery warm. Latest global ensembles show 850mb temps cooling by \nonly a degree or two centigrade into Friday, which should only\nresult in 1-4 degrees of surface cooling from Thursday. Latest\nhigh temperature forecast for Friday has 101-106 degrees across\nsoutheast CA and southwest AZ to 97-101 degrees across south-\ncentral AZ.\n\nAs alluded to, the pattern will shift heading into the end of this\nweek and this weekend with a trend toward troffing and negative\nheight anomalies across the Western CONUS. While there is good\nconfidence in the trough pattern developing, there is still a lot\nof model uncertainty/spread regarding how the trough pattern will\nevolve heading into the end of the weekend and start of next week.\nIt does look like the Pacific low that will near the CA coast on\nFriday will dip south this weekend and interact or faze with TC \nRachel while at the same time a trough will move out of the Gulf\nof Alaska and strengthen as it moves south down the northern\nPacific coast. There are certainly a lot of eyes on what will\nhappen with TC Rachel and whether or not it will help bring rain\nto the Desert Southwest, just like with TCs Odalys and Polo early\nlast week. Latest guidance has trended toward an eventual turn to\nthe northeast by Rachel by the end of the weekend. While this\ntrend does lend itself in favor of a push of moisture toward the\nSouthwest CONUS, there is still a lot of uncertainty in the\nforecast this far out, especially with timing and evolution of the\ntrough moving down the northern Pacific coast and how that may\naffect the remnants of Rachel. Current thinking is at least mid \nand high level moisture will eventually shear off or Rachel up \ntoward the Southwest, which may bring showery activity as early \nas Sunday and then the more potent coastal trough/low will help\ndrive additional shower and storm chances across AZ, while also\nsending a cold front and cooler temperatures through the region\nearly next week. Again uncertainty is still high and it is ill-\nadvised to buy into single deterministic long-range model runs\nthat may show a strong tropical circulation pushing into the \nU.S.. Stay tuned for forecast updates through the week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1700Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNO major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nLingering breeziness will be on the downtrend and should become\nmore sporadic during the afternoon and evening. Easterly flow \nwill once again persist through the forecast window. Other than a \nFEW mid-level cloud decks around 15 kft over the southern part of \nthe airspace, skies will be mostly clear.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation concerns are expected over the next 24 hours. Winds\nwill switch from a NE component this afternoon toward the NW by\nthis evening with periods of VRB conditions overnight. Mostly\nclear skies will prevail across the region.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with well above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough the week, with afternoon high temperatures reaching up to\n100-107 degrees across the lower deserts (hottest on Thursday). \nBreezy easterly winds will continue across southern AZ through \nthis afternoon and Tuesday morning, with gusts up to 20-25 mph. \nWinds will return to their more typical light diurnal tendencies  \nTuesday afternoon through Thursday, with only minor afternoon \nupslope gustiness. A gusty easterly gradient wind is expected to\ndevelop again by Friday and last through Saturday. MinRHs will \nmostly range from 15-25% across the western districts to 20-35% \nacross eastern districts through the week with fair to good \novernight recovery (35- 60%) area-wide. Humidities will be lowest\non Thursday when temperatures are likely at their hottest, but\nwinds will still be light at this time, so there are no elevated \nfire weather concerns. No wetting rain expected through at least \nSaturday. Rain chances then go up by the end of the weekend/start\nof next week with increased moisture and a potential weather\nsystem moving through.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for AZZ532.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for CAZ566-567.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict\nAVIATION...RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict\n",
